SourceCode.Declaration:
/// <summary>
/// Creates the DMF definition groups counting-journal lines flow through, entirely by code, on
/// first use. Both target `InventInventoryCountingJournalLineEntity`.
///
/// **Export creates a new definition group for every request** (`ensureExportForJournal`, unique
/// name each call) - the strongest form of the per-journal isolation described next. Historical
/// context follows.
///
/// **Export is one definition group per journal, not one shared group.** The first version
/// shared a single `HSMobileOpsCountingExport` project across every journal, scoped by rewriting
/// its packed `QueryData` per request - which broke under two workers requesting *different*
/// journals close together (both rewrite the same row; whichever write lands last wins) and,
/// even after being widened to scope to every open journal at once to close that race, still
/// left concurrent *executions* sharing the same project's staging infrastructure underneath the
/// filter - the same category of risk Microsoft's own docs flag for `ImportFromPackage` ("don't
/// call this in parallel"), just undocumented for the synchronous `exportToFile` path this app
/// uses. A project per journal (`ensureExportForJournal`) removes the shared state entirely:
/// nothing two concurrent requests for different journals can collide on, because each gets its
/// own project. Only became practical once `ensureDefinitionGroup`/`ensureEntityInGroup` below
/// were confirmed reliable - creating one project used to be the riskiest, most-guessed part of
/// this whole model; creating N of them was out of the question until then.
///
/// **Import stays on one shared project** (`HSMobileOpsCountingImport`) - see
/// `HSMobileOpsCountingService::importCounts`'s own doc comment for why: it moved to
/// `DMFPackageImporter`'s enqueue-a-batch-job pattern, which gets its concurrency safety from
/// D365's own batch framework rather than from project isolation, so it doesn't need the same
/// per-request fix export does.
///
/// Rebuilt around confirmed production APIs found in a real, shipped integration class
/// (`DataManagementIntegrationUtils`, from the Personnel/HCM integration module) rather than the
/// raw `DmfDefinitionGroup`/`DmfDefinitionGroupEntity` table inserts this class started with.
/// That reference is what unblocked two separate live failures:
///
/// - `DmfDefinitionGroup.OperationType` is the real export/import direction field - on the
///   definition group (parent), not the entity row, contrary to this class's own first guess.
///   `Adapter` and `GenerateDataPackage` are two more real fields the earlier raw insert never
///   set at all.
/// - `DMFEntityBase::addEntityForProcessingV3(...)` is the real, complete "add this entity to
///   this project" API. It handles field mapping and whatever registration
///   `DMFEntityExporter::exportToFile` needs internally - confirmed missing before:
///   `exportToFile` threw `Entity 'InventInventoryCountingJournalLineEntity' not found` against
///   a project whose entity row this class had inserted by hand, with no mapping generated at
///   all. One call replaces this class's whole earlier guess-one-field-at-a-time approach.
///
/// **What's confirmed vs guessed in the `addEntityForProcessingV3` call, precisely**: the
/// reference passes twelve positional arguments; this class mirrors all twelve exactly as shown
/// there, substituting only this app's own definition group/entity names. Which of the
/// unlabelled `true`/`false`/`''` arguments matter for *this* entity, versus being
/// Personnel-integration-specific defaults that happen to work here too, is not independently
/// verified - reproduced faithfully because guessing new values at any of those positions would
/// be a worse bet than reusing values known to work in real, shipped code.
/// </summary>
class HSMobileOpsCountingExportSetup
{
}

Method.Source:
/// <summary>
/// Creates a brand-new export definition group for this one request and attaches the
/// counting-journal-line entity to it - a new project on every call, never reused, so
/// concurrent requests (different journals, or even the same journal) can never share a
/// project, its packed query, or its staging state, and a project left half-configured by an
/// earlier failed attempt can never poison a later one. Returns the group's name, ready for
/// HSMobileOpsCountingService to scope and export it. Projects accumulate (one per request)
/// and are not removed here.
/// </summary>
public static DMFDefinitionGroupName ensureExportForJournal(InventJournalId _journalId)
{
    DMFDefinitionGroupName groupName = HSMobileOpsCountingExportSetup::exportDefinitionGroupName(_journalId);

    HSMobileOpsCountingExportSetup::ensureDefinitionGroup(groupName, DMFOperationType::Export);
    HSMobileOpsCountingExportSetup::ensureEntityInGroup(groupName);

    return groupName;
}

/// <summary>
/// Creates (or confirms) the shared counted-quantity-back import definition group - see this
/// class's own doc comment on why import stays on one shared project while export does not.
/// Called from `HSMobileOpsCountingService::importCounts` before every enqueue.
/// </summary>
public static void ensureImport()
{
    HSMobileOpsCountingExportSetup::ensureDefinitionGroup(
        HSMobileOpsCountingExportSetup::importDefinitionGroupName(), DMFOperationType::Import);
    HSMobileOpsCountingExportSetup::ensureEntityInGroup(
        HSMobileOpsCountingExportSetup::importDefinitionGroupName());
}

/// <summary>
/// `DataManagementIntegrationUtils::createDefinitionGroup`'s own pattern, confirmed from
/// real shipped code: `Adapter`, `DefinitionGroupName`, `Description`, `OperationType`,
/// `GenerateDataPackage` are the fields it sets - none of them guessed this time, all copied
/// from a working reference. The reference also calls `DMFParameters::find(false, true,
/// false)` after creating a group ("make sure the entity and data source types tables have
/// been populated before attempting to use them") - confirmed live to be what actually
/// registers an entity into DMF's own internal catalog (`DMFEntity`), which this class's
/// earlier raw insert never did and which `exportToFile`'s "Entity not found" pointed at.
/// </summary>
private static void ensureDefinitionGroup(DMFDefinitionGroupName _groupName, DMFOperationType _direction)
{
    DmfDefinitionGroup definitionGroup = DmfDefinitionGroup::find(_groupName);

    if (!definitionGroup.RecId)
    {
        ttsbegin;

        definitionGroup.clear();
        definitionGroup.Adapter              = DMFAdapter::SSISAdapter;
        definitionGroup.DefinitionGroupName  = _groupName;
        definitionGroup.Description          = _groupName;
        definitionGroup.OperationType        = _direction;
        definitionGroup.GenerateDataPackage  = true;
        definitionGroup.insert();

        ttscommit;

        info(strFmt('Created DmfDefinitionGroup %1 (%2).', _groupName, enum2Str(_direction)));
    }

    DMFParameters::find(false, true, false);
}

/// <summary>
/// `DataManagementIntegrationUtils::addEntityToDefinitionGroup`'s own pattern, confirmed
/// from real shipped code - `DMFEntityBase::addEntityForProcessingV3` in place of a raw
/// `DmfDefinitionGroupEntity` insert. See this class's own doc comment on exactly what is
/// and is not independently confirmed about the positional arguments below.
///
/// **The entity name passed here must be DMF's own name for it, not the AOT name.** This is
/// what kept every earlier attempt failing: `DmfDefinitionGroupEntity.Entity` holds
/// `DMFEntity.EntityName` (`Inventory counting journal lines`), and rows written with the AOT
/// name (`InventInventoryCountingJournalLineEntity`) were invisible to the Data Management UI
/// and to DMF itself - `Entity ... not found`, or an export stuck at `NotRun`. See
/// [entityName]. The source is `'CSV'` (a real `DMFDataSource` row, matching the format this
/// app parses) and `SkipStaging` is false, matching the row the UI's own "Add entity" wrote.
/// </summary>
private static void ensureEntityInGroup(DMFDefinitionGroupName _groupName)
{
    str entityName = HSMobileOpsCountingExportSetup::entityName();
    DmfDefinitionGroupEntity existing = DMFDefinitionGroupEntity::find(_groupName, entityName);

    if (existing.RecId)
    {
        if (existing.Source == 'CSV')
        {
            return;
        }

        // Self-heals a row this method itself left behind before the source was corrected
        // from 'XML-Element' to 'CSV' - without this, the find() above would keep finding
        // the same stale, mismatched-source row forever and never re-add it correctly, the
        // same trap this class hit once already with a whole stale definition group. Safe
        // to delete outright: this row only ever exists because this method created it.
        str staleSource = existing.Source;

        ttsbegin;
        existing.selectForUpdate(true);
        existing.delete();
        ttscommit;

        info(strFmt('Removed stale %1/%2 registration (Source was %3) before re-adding.',
            _groupName, entityName, staleSource));
    }

    DMFEntityBase::addEntityForProcessingV3(
        _groupName,
        _groupName,
        entityName,
        'CSV',
        '',
        '@SYS6047',
        false,
        true,
        '',
        false,
        DMFRefreshType::FullPush,
        false);

    info(strFmt('Added %1 to %2.', entityName, _groupName));
}

/// <summary>
/// Convenience entry point for running this by hand from Visual Studio (right-click > Run).
/// Only ensures the shared import project - export projects are per-journal now and don't
/// exist to pre-create; kept only because a job needs an Args-taking static method to show
/// up as runnable in the designer.
/// </summary>
public static void run(Args _args)
{
    HSMobileOpsCountingExportSetup::ensureImport();

    info(strFmt('Done. %1 exists.', HSMobileOpsCountingExportSetup::importDefinitionGroupName()));
}

/// <summary>
/// A new, unique name on every call - `HCE_<last 12 chars of journal id>_<8 chars of a
/// fresh GUID>` - because every "Get data" request gets its own project (see
/// [ensureExportForJournal]). Short on purpose: `DmfDefinitionGroupName`'s real maximum
/// length is unconfirmed, so this keeps to 25 characters, well under any plausible limit
/// (`HCE_JN_000552000` at 16 characters is the longest name confirmed to work live).
/// </summary>
public static DMFDefinitionGroupName exportDefinitionGroupName(InventJournalId _journalId)
{
    str sanitized = strReplace(_journalId, '-', '_');
    str journalPart = strLen(sanitized) > 12 ? subStr(sanitized, strLen(sanitized) - 11, 12) : sanitized;
    str uniquePart = subStr(strRem(guid2Str(newGuid()), '{}-'), 1, 8);

    return 'HCE_' + journalPart + '_' + uniquePart;
}

/// <summary>
/// The entity's name *as DMF knows it* - `DMFEntity.EntityName`, which for this entity is the
/// label `Inventory counting journal lines`, not the AOT name
/// [aotEntityName]. `DmfDefinitionGroupEntity.Entity` holds the DMF name: confirmed live by
/// dumping the rows of a UI-built project next to code-built ones - the UI's own row carried
/// `Inventory counting journal lines` and was the only one that showed in the "Selected
/// entities" grid and the only one that ever exported, while every row this class wrote with
/// the AOT name was invisible to the UI and to DMF (`Entity ... not found`, exports stuck at
/// `NotRun`). Looked up from the AOT name via `DMFEntity.TargetEntity` rather than hardcoded
/// so it follows whatever this environment calls it.
/// </summary>
public static str entityName()
{
    DMFEntity dmfEntity;

    select firstOnly EntityName from dmfEntity
        where dmfEntity.TargetEntity == HSMobileOpsCountingExportSetup::aotEntityName();

    if (!dmfEntity.EntityName)
    {
        throw error(strFmt(
            'DMF has no entity whose target is %1 - is it enabled for this environment?',
            HSMobileOpsCountingExportSetup::aotEntityName()));
    }

    return dmfEntity.EntityName;
}
